  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Warm and breezy today ahead of an afternoon/early evening\ncold front.\n\nBreezy westerly winds will continue as a cold front pushes off the \ncoast later this afternoon. Gusts 25-30 mph will be common through \nthe late afternoon while surface temps warm into the mid-upper 70s \none last day this week. Strong cold air advection will occur in the \nwake of the front overnight with a west-northwest flow bringing \nnoticeably cooler air into the local area. This could maintain gusty \nconditions into the mid evening hours, but a notable decrease in low-\nlvl wind fields is expected through the night post fropa.\n\nLake Winds: Breezy west-southwesterly winds across Lake \nMoultrie will peak around 25-30 kt through late afternoon. Low-\nlvl wind fields will support favorable mixing with the passage \nof the cold front, trending weaker by sunset. A brief Lake Wind \nAdvisory has been issued for Lake Moultrie until this evening.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Frost/freeze conditions are possible Friday night \nthrough Sunday night.\n\nA dry cold front will sweep across the region today. Following the \ncold front, steady H85 CAA is forecast to occur until 12Z Saturday, \nwith values between -1 to -2C. Using a blend of guidance, low \ntemperatures Saturday morning are forecast to range from the mid 30s \ninland to the low to mid 40s across the coastal counties. Northwest \nwinds Friday night into Saturday morning should range between 5 to 8 \nmph with RH below 95 percent. Given marginal temperatures, winds, \nand RH frost may only form in sheltered locations or rooftops across \ninland counties Saturday morning. The forecast will mention areas to \npatchy frost in the most favorable locations.\n\nChilly llvl thicknesses will remain on Saturday, with highs ranging \naround 60 degrees. The center of 1030 mb high over the Mid West will \nridge SE across GA and SC Saturday night into Sunday morning. The \nweakening pressure gradient should yield light to calm winds through \nmuch of Saturday night. Given a clear sky, light to calm winds, and \ndeep dry air, radiational cooling conditions should provide the \nlowest temperatures so far this season. Favoring values near the \n10th percentile of the guidance, low temperatures are forecast to \nfall below freezing inland, generally west of I-95. Frost supporting \ntemperatures are possible into portions of the coastal counties. A \nFreeze Watch may be needed for a portion of the inland forecast \narea. If temperature exceed the currently forecasted values, then \nthe low temperatures on Sunday morning could end the growing season \nfor the region. The forecast will indicate widespread to areas of \nfrost Sunday morning.\n\nThe cool air mass will linger into early next week. Frost supporting \ntemperature are possible inland again Monday morning. By Tuesday \ninto Wednesday morning, temperatures should remain in the upper 30s \nfor the coolest locations.",
